1e:I[470545,["/_next/static/chunks/9f06f549534c8a5e.js","/_next/static/chunks/276fa16bd4cf020e.js","/_next/static/chunks/87128f2527464d5c.js","/_next/static/chunks/2d904702e4d1b18c.js","/_next/static/chunks/b1a4b4a170e41847.js","/_next/static/chunks/f9b9d3a69abee8ee.js","/_next/static/chunks/358e760bd3164198.js","/_next/static/chunks/ea566679d384b192.js","/_next/static/chunks/7fae7bca3e4de959.js","/_next/static/chunks/eac00b17670dc9a8.js","/_next/static/chunks/5b7085386fe8ed14.js","/_next/static/chunks/079f70de56a2a4d9.js","/_next/static/chunks/a0c10228c37fdc14.js","/_next/static/chunks/8480234093eeb56c.js","/_next/static/chunks/c73abd7be8c311cf.js","/_next/static/chunks/adf525ed1f944be0.js","/_next/static/chunks/ed17766d5eadb297.js","/_next/static/chunks/65d84b2239f181ae.js","/_next/static/chunks/af1c46f0af80dd0c.js","/_next/static/chunks/a05dae2f3292eb60.js","/_next/static/chunks/19913707827f8dfb.js"],"default"]
7:["$","$L17",null,{"maxWidth":"lg","sx":{"my":4},"children":["$L18",["$","$L19",null,{"variant":"h2","component":"h1","color":"primary","gutterBottom":true,"children":"Module A0: Before you start"}],"$undefined",["$","$L1a",null,{"sx":{"my":2}}],["$","$L1b",null,{"children":[["$","$L1c",null,{"label":"Overview and prerequisites","children":["$","$L1d",null,{"sx":[{"& p":{"my":1,"lineHeight":1.7},"& p:first-of-type":{"mt":0},"& ul, & ol":{"pl":3,"my":1},"& li":{"my":0.5},"& code":{"backgroundColor":"rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.06)","borderRadius":"4px","px":"4px","fontSize":"0.9em"}},{"color":"text.secondary","& p":{"textAlign":"justify"}}],"dangerouslySetInnerHTML":{"__html":"<p>Three short reading steps (about 10 minutes) to set the vocabulary before tackling GRAFCET in module A1. Nothing to build here, just enough to understand what you&#39;ll observe and manipulate next.</p>\n"}}]}],["$","$L1e",null,{"steps":[{"id":"automatisme","kind":"theory","title":"What is an automated system?","body":["Take an automatic car park barrier: a car pulls up, a device detects it, and the barrier lifts on its own, without an operator pressing a button. That's an automated system: something that constantly watches its surroundings and acts based on what it observes, following logic decided in advance. A traffic light cycling through its three colours, a conveyor belt sorting boxes by size are other examples. By contrast, a barrier lifted by hand with a key is not an automated system: an operator decides every time.","Whatever \"watches\" (the detection device at the park entrance) is called a sensor; whatever \"acts\" (the motor that lifts the barrier) is called an actuator. Before wiring or programming anything, it helps to describe that expected behaviour precisely (what to do, in what order, under what conditions), independent of the equipment that will later run it (a PLC, a computer, relays). That's exactly the role of GRAFCET, which you'll discover in module A1: a way of drawing the expected behaviour, like a plan before building.","<div style=\"text-align:center\"><figure><img src=\"/training/automatisme-chaine-en.svg\" width=\"560\" alt=\"Chain of an automated system: the car detector (sensor) sends information to the PLC, which sends commands to the barrier motor (actuator)\" /></figure></div>","The PLC (programmable logic controller, called an \"automate\" in French) is the small industrial computer that receives information from the sensors, runs the logic and drives the actuators.","Once that behaviour is described in GRAFCET, it still needs to become executable and controllable. Studomate lets you work with three languages on the same project, sharing the same variables between them:\n\n- **GRAFCET: describe.** The expected behaviour, step by step (module A1). Example: the barrier opens when a car is detected.\n- **Ladder: program.** The program a PLC runs (modules A2 to A5). It looks like relay diagrams, relays being electrical components that open or close a circuit. Example: a \"car detected\" contact driving the \"lift the barrier\" coil.\n- **HMI: supervise.** The human-machine interface is the screen through which an operator watches and drives the automated system once it is running (module A5, coming soon). Example: the \"barrier open\" indicator and the manual open button.","**Key takeaway:** a sensor observes, an actuator acts, a PLC decides."]},{"id":"logic-basics","kind":"theory","title":"Variables and basic logic","body":["A variable is a named memory cell holding a value that can change. Back to the barrier: at any instant, either a car is detected or it isn't: two possible states. That's what's called a boolean variable: true or false (0 or 1; in Studomate, you write TRUE or FALSE). The sensor that detects the car is a boolean variable you read (an input), the command that lifts the barrier is one you write (an output); some variables are only used internally by the logic, never touching the field. A counter of free spaces, on the other hand, isn't limited to two states: that's a numeric variable.","<div style=\"text-align:center\"><figure><img src=\"/training/variables-entree-sortie-en.svg\" width=\"560\" alt=\"Variables in the automation chain: car_detected is an input that is read, lift_barrier an output that is written, barrier_up an internal memory of the PLC, spaces a numeric variable\" /></figure></div>","Combining several conditions only takes three words, and they read like plain English:\n\n- the door is closed AND the button is pressed (both are needed at once)\n- emergency_stop OR fault (either one is enough, including both together)\n- NOT presence (the opposite of a condition)","<div style=\"text-align:center\"><figure><img src=\"/training/logique-et-ou-non-en.svg\" width=\"560\" alt=\"AND: two switches in series, the lamp lights if both are closed. OR: two switches in parallel, the lamp lights if at least one is closed. NOT: a normally closed contact, the lamp lights when it is not actuated. Matching truth tables.\" /></figure></div>","A number can also be compared, with =, !=, <, >, <= or >=, such as spaces < 5. An expression is a condition written with variables and these words, for example the one on a transition in module A1. In Studomate, expressions are written like this:\n\n- `door_closed AND button`\n- `NOT presence`\n- `spaces < 5`\n\nA variable name contains no spaces: write `door_closed`, with an underscore.","Test yourself:\n\n1. Barrier: `car_detected AND spaces > 0`. What does the expression equal if `spaces` is 0?\n2. `emergency_stop OR fault`. What does the expression equal if both variables are true?","<details><summary>Show the answers</summary><ol><li>False: <code>spaces > 0</code> is false, and AND requires both conditions to be true.</li><li>True: OR is true as soon as at least one of the two conditions is, including both together.</li></ol></details>","Good to know: Studomate accepts these words in both French (ET, OU, NON, VRAI, FAUX) and English (AND, OR, NOT, TRUE, FALSE), depending on the dialect chosen for the project. The two notations aren't mixed within the same project.","**Key takeaway:** a boolean variable is true or false; AND requires both conditions, OR at least one of them, NOT reverses a condition."]},{"id":"scan-cycle","kind":"theory","title":"The PLC and its scan cycle","body":["A PLC doesn't react instantly: like a guard walking a round in a loop, it looks at everything, decides, then acts, and starts over. This is called the scan cycle. The PLC's inputs are the sensors, its outputs the actuators. On every pass, very fast, it chains three steps:\n\n1. read the state of every input;\n2. run the program to decide the outputs;\n3. write those outputs, then start over immediately.","<div style=\"text-align:center\"><figure><img src=\"/training/scan-cycle-en.svg\" width=\"560\" alt=\"Scan cycle: read the inputs, run the program, write the outputs, then start again, illustrated with a traffic light\" /></figure></div>","So a traffic light doesn't constantly \"know\" it's green: on every pass, it rechecks its timer and decides whether to change colour or stay as it is. Studomate's simulation reproduces this cycle.","Preview of module A1: this loop gives full meaning to notions you'll meet there, such as running an action \"continuously\" (recomputed every pass of the loop) or \"on an edge\" (just once, on the pass where a condition turns true).","A GRAFCET is made of numbered steps (X0, X1, X2...); a step is active (true) or inactive (false). There's nothing more to understand for now: just watch which step is active when the light changes.","Step by step:\n\n1. Click \"Watch the simulation\": the simulation starts automatically.\n2. In the GRAFCET, spot the active step (highlighted): its step variable Xn is true.\n3. In the supervision screen (HMI), watch the indicator switching on and off in rhythm.\n4. When the indicator changes, the active step changes too: the previous step's variable Xn turns false.","<div style=\"text-align:center\"><figure><img src=\"/training/traffic-light-simulation-en.svg\" width=\"560\" alt=\"What to watch in the simulation: on the left, the traffic light GRAFCET, with steps 0, 1 and 2 linked in a loop; step 1 is active (X1 = TRUE) and its action drives the amber output. Below the step, the transition waits for the timer to run out. On the right, the supervision screen (HMI) shows the amber indicator on, red and green off.\" /></figure></div>","**Key takeaway:** a PLC repeats a loop of three steps: read the inputs, run the program, write the outputs."],"embeddedTemplateId":"traffic-light","cta":{"templateId":"traffic-light","exerciseLabel":"Watch the simulation","primaryMode":"solution","autostartSimulation":true}},{"id":"conclusion","kind":"theory","title":"Conclusion","body":["You now know what an automation system is and how a PLC runs its program. Take stock, notion by notion:\n\n- **sensor and actuator**: in the parking barrier, which is the sensor, which is the actuator, and what makes the decision?\n- **variables**: `voiture_detectee`, `lever_barriere` and `barriere_levee`: which one is an input, which an output, which an internal memory?\n- **logic**: what does `voiture_detectee AND NOT barriere_levee` evaluate to when a car is detected and the barrier is already raised?\n- **scan cycle**: in which order does the PLC chain its three steps, and why does a traffic light \"recheck\" its timer on every pass?","<details><summary>Show the answers</summary><ol><li>The car detector is the sensor, the barrier motor is the actuator, and the PLC makes the decision.</li><li><code>voiture_detectee</code> is an input (read), <code>lever_barriere</code> an output (written), <code>barriere_levee</code> a memory internal to the PLC.</li><li>False: the car is detected (true), but <code>NOT barriere_levee</code> is false since the barrier is raised, and AND requires both conditions to be true.</li><li>Read the inputs, run the program, write the outputs, then start over immediately. A PLC doesn't react continuously: on every pass, it rereads the state of its inputs (here, the timer) and decides whether to change colour or stay as it is.</li></ol></details>","What remains is to describe precisely the expected behaviour of an automation system before programming it. That is the purpose of module A1, which introduces GRAFCET: the numbered steps (X0, X1, X2...) you watched in the traffic light simulation."]}],"prevLabel":"Previous","nextLabel":"Next","moduleId":"a0","initialStepId":"$undefined","nextModule":{"href":"/training/a1","label":"Move on to module A1"}}]]}],"$L1f","$L20"]}]
